{
  "dom_id": "step-q345391",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{\u2217}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{\u2217}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$",
  "worked_solution": "According to Newton\u2019s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{\u2217}=2\\,\\text{s}.$",
